; ry: auto; scale: none; speak: normal; stroke: none; transform: none; transition: all; translate: none; visibility: visible; x: 0px; y: 0px; zoom: 1; margin-top: 0px !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;&quot;&gt;The Hallucination Trap:&lt;/b&gt; If you write a naive script to auto-generate instructions from raw text, the LLM frequently hallucinates bad syntax, incorrect API signatures, or broken code snippets. Training an adapter on synthetic garbage destroys its downstream performance.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;animation: auto ease 0s 1 normal none running none; appearance: none; background: none 0% 0% / auto repeat scroll padding-box border-box rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); border: 0px rgb(31, 31, 31); inset: 0px; clear: none; clip: auto; columns: auto; contain: none; container: none; content: normal; cursor: auto; cx: 0px; cy: 0px; d: none; direction: ltr; fill: rgb(0, 0, 0); filter: none; flex: 0 1 auto; flex-direction: row; float: none; gap: normal; hyphens: manual; interactivity: auto; isolation: auto; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px; margin-left: 0px; marker: none; mask: none; offset: normal; opacity: 1; order: 0; outline: rgb(31, 31, 31) none 2.66667px; overlay: none; padding: 0px 0px 0px 36px; page: auto; perspective: none; position: relative; quotes: auto; r: 0px; resize: none; rotate: none; rx: auto; ry: auto; scale: none; speak: normal; stroke: none; transform: none; transition: all; translate: none; visibility: visible; x: 0px; y: 0px; zoom: 1; margin-top: 0px !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;animation: auto ease 0s 1 normal none running none; appearance: none; background: none 0% 0% / auto repeat scroll padding-box border-box rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); border: 0px rgb(31, 31, 31); inset: auto; clear: none; clip: auto; columns: auto; contain: none; container: none; content: normal; cursor: auto; cx: 0px; cy: 0px; d: none; direction: ltr; fill: rgb(0, 0, 0); filter: none; flex: 0 1 auto; flex-direction: row; float: none; gap: normal; hyphens: manual; interactivity: auto; isolation: auto; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px; margin-left: 0px; marker: none; mask: none; offset: normal; opacity: 1; order: 0; outline: rgb(31, 31, 31) none 2.66667px; overlay: none; padding: 0px 0px 0px 4px; page: auto; perspective: none; position: static; quotes: auto; r: 0px; resize: none; rotate: none; rx: auto; ry: auto; scale: none; speak: normal; stroke: none; transform: none; transition: all; translate: none; visibility: visible; x: 0px; y: 0px; zoom: 1; margin-top: 0px !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;6,2,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;animation: auto ease 0s 1 normal none running none; appearance: none; background: none 0% 0% / auto repeat scroll padding-box border-box rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); border: 0px rgb(31, 31, 31); inset: auto; clear: none; clip: auto; columns: auto; contain: none; container: none; content: normal; cursor: auto; cx: 0px; cy: 0px; d: none; direction: ltr; display: inline; fill: rgb(0, 0, 0); filter: none; flex: 0 1 auto; flex-direction: row; float: none; gap: normal; hyphens: manual; interactivity: auto; isolation: auto; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px; margin-left: 0px; marker: none; mask: none; offset: normal; opacity: 1; order: 0; outline: rgb(31, 31, 31) none 2.66667px; overlay: none; padding: 0px; page: auto; perspective: none; position: static; quotes: auto; r: 0px; resize: none; rotate: none; rx: auto; ry: auto; scale: none; speak: normal; stroke: none; transform: none; transition: all; translate: none; visibility: visible; x: 0px; y: 0px; zoom: 1; margin-top: 0px !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;&quot;&gt;Pipeline Maintenance Overhead:&lt;/b&gt; Building an agentic pipeline that reliably slices documentation, strips noise, formats valid JSON/chat templates, and cross-references facts against a local corpus requires real software engineering. Most teams default to manual prompt engineering or generic datasets because it&#39;s easier.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;h3 data-path-to-node=&quot;7&quot; style=&quot;animation: auto ease 0s 1 normal none running none; appearance: none; background: none 0% 0% / auto repeat scroll padding-box border-box rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); border: 0px rgb(31, 31, 31); inset: auto; clear: none; clip: auto; columns: auto; contain: none; container: none; content: normal; cursor: auto; cx: 0px; cy: 0px; d: none; direction: ltr; fill: rgb(0, 0, 0); filter: none; flex: 0 1 auto; flex-direction: row; float: none; gap: normal; hyphens: manual; interactivity: auto; isolation: auto; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px; margin-left: 0px; marker: none; mask: none; offset: normal; opacity: 1; order: 0; outline: rgb(31, 31, 31) none 2.66667px; overlay: none; padding: 0px; page: auto; perspective: none; position: static; quotes: auto; r: 0px; resize: none; rotate: none; rx: auto; ry: auto; scale: none; speak: normal; stroke: none; transform: none; transition: all; translate: none; visibility: visible; x: 0px; y: 0px; zoom: 1; margin-top: 0px !important; font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;&quot;&gt;Why it earns the &lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;7&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;17&quot; style=&quot;animation: auto ease 0s 1 normal none running none; appearance: none; background: none 0% 0% / auto repeat scroll padding-box border-box rgb(242, 240, 240); border: 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55); inset: auto; clear: none; clip: auto; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55); columns: auto; contain: none; container: none; content: normal; cursor: auto; cx: 0px; cy: 0px; d: none; direction: ltr; display: inline-block; fill: rgb(0, 0, 0); filter: none; flex: 0 1 auto; flex-direction: row; float: none; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-weight: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 15px; gap: normal; hyphens: manual; interactivity: auto; isolation: auto; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px; margin-left: 0px; marker: none; mask: none; offset: normal; opacity: 1; order: 0; outline: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55) none 2.66667px; overlay: none; padding: 4px 6px; page: auto; perspective: none; position: static; quotes: auto; r: 0px; resize: none; rotate: none; rx: auto; ry: auto; scale: none; speak: normal; stroke: none; transform: none; transition: all; translate: none; visibility: visible; x: 0px; y: 0px; zoom: 1; line-height: 1.15 !important; font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;🔥&lt;/code&gt; (Applied Practice) tag&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;animation: auto ease 0s 1 normal none running none; appearance: none; background: none 0% 0% / auto repeat scroll padding-box border-box rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); border: 0px rgb(31, 31, 31); inset: auto; clear: none; clip: auto; columns: auto; contain: none; container: none; content: normal; cursor: auto; cx: 0px; cy: 0px; d: none; direction: ltr; fill: rgb(0, 0, 0); filter: none; flex: 0 1 auto; flex-direction: row; float: none; gap: normal; hyphens: manual; interactivity: auto; isolation: auto; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px; margin-left: 0px; marker: none; mask: none; offset: normal; opacity: 1; order: 0; outline: rgb(31, 31, 31) none 2.66667px; overlay: none; padding: 0px; page: auto; perspective: none; position: static; quotes: auto; r: 0px; resize: none; rotate: none; rx: auto; ry: auto; scale: none; speak: normal; stroke: none; transform: none; transition: all; translate: none; visibility: visible; x: 0px; y: 0px; zoom: 1; margin-top: 0px !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;&quot;&gt;It takes a classical software engineering pattern—&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;8&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;50&quot; style=&quot;animation: auto ease 0s 1 normal none running none; appearance: none; background: none 0% 0% / auto repeat scroll padding-box border-box rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); border: 0px rgb(31, 31, 31); inset: auto; clear: none; clip: auto; columns: auto; contain: none; container: none; content: normal; cursor: auto; cx: 0px; cy: 0px; d: none; direction: ltr; display: inline; fill: rgb(0, 0, 0); filter: none; flex: 0 1 auto; flex-direction: row; float: none; gap: normal; hyphens: manual; interactivity: auto; isolation: auto; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px; margin-left: 0px; marker: none; mask: none; offset: normal; opacity: 1; order: 0; outline: rgb(31, 31, 31) none 2.66667px; overlay: none; padding: 0px; page: auto; perspective: none; position: static; quotes: auto; r: 0px; resize: none; rotate: none; rx: auto; ry: auto; scale: none; speak: normal; stroke: none; transform: none; transition: all; translate: none; visibility: visible; x: 0px; y: 0px; zoom: 1; margin-top: 0px !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;&quot;&gt;automated data transformation pipelines&lt;/b&gt;—and applies it to solve an active LLM bottleneck: feeding a multi-expert factory with clean, domain-specific instruction pairs without requiring manual human annotation for every new codebase or repository you want your agent to master.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="890" width="385" x="575" y="840" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="QsjXB6iZQFBQTkZlhdo4-6" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;🚀FlashNorm-Style Weight Folding (The Forward Pass Speedup)&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;h2 data-path-to-node=&quot;2&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;1. Executive Summary&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;3&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;FlashNorm-style weight folding eliminates RMSNorm kernel launch overhead at decode time by mathematically absorbing pre-normalization scale parameters (&lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;\gamma&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;152&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$\gamma$&lt;/span&gt;) directly into downstream linear projection weights (&lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;W_{\text{folded}} = (1 + \gamma) \cdot W&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;212&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$W_{\text{folded}} = (1 + \gamma) \cdot W$&lt;/span&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;4&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;While weight folding as a conceptual primitive is an established optimization pattern in static neural network compilation, its implementation in an advanced, dynamic architecture featuring runtime adapters, state-space models (SSMs), and non-standard hardware (AMD ROCm &lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;4&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;271&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;gfx1100&lt;/code&gt;) required solving complex inter-module dependencies. On this stack, the optimization yielded a &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;4&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;374&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;7.75% end-to-end throughput savings&lt;/b&gt;, eliminated individual normalization kernel launches (saving &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;4&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;471&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;6.60 µs per norm&lt;/b&gt; across 73 norms), delivered a &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;4&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;518&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;+2.4% to +3.1% decode velocity bump&lt;/b&gt;, and maintained an exact numerical preservation match (relative L2 divergence of &lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;2.40 \times 10^{-7}&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;635&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$2.40 \times 10^{-7}$&lt;/span&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h2 data-path-to-node=&quot;6&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;2. Why This Earned a &lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;6&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;21&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;🚀&lt;/code&gt; (Genuine Discovery / High-Value Spike)&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;7&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;In your internal legend, standard engineering patterns are classified as Industry Standard (&lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;7&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;92&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;⭐&lt;/code&gt;), while advanced external concepts adapted to solve uniquely hostile runtime or hardware problems earn the Applied Practice (&lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;7&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;219&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;🔥&lt;/code&gt;) tag. FlashNorm-Style Weight Folding earned the rare &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;7&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;275&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;🚀&lt;/b&gt; rating because it bridged a critical gap between static weight compilation and dynamic, multi-adapter agentic execution.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;8&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Standard model weight loaders assume static parameters. Implementing this optimization required inventing a clean bypass for dynamic architectural elements that would otherwise break standard folding logic—specifically, preventing runtime adapter degradation and managing complex multi-fan-out topologies without incurring numerical drift.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h2 data-path-to-node=&quot;10&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;3. Why Wasn&#39;t This Standard Practice Before?&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;11&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;The natural question is: &lt;i data-path-to-node=&quot;11&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;25&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;If weight folding is mathematically straightforward, why isn&#39;t it a default feature in mainstream frameworks like Hugging Face Transformers or vLLM?&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;12&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;The answer lies in three major engineering blind spots that make naive weight folding destructive in advanced stacks:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h3 data-path-to-node=&quot;13&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;A. The Adapter Interoperability Barrier (The LoRA/Expert Collision)&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;14&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;In modern modular setups, base weights are dynamically augmented by low-rank adaptation matrices or expert deltas (&lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;W_{\text{effective}} = W_{\text{base}} + s \cdot (U @ V)&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;115&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$W_{\text{effective}} = W_{\text{base}} + s \cdot (U @ V)$&lt;/span&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;ul data-path-to-node=&quot;15&quot; style=&quot;padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;15,0,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;15,0,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;The Conflict:&lt;/b&gt; If you fold the RMSNorm scale factor (&lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;\gamma&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;52&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$\gamma$&lt;/span&gt;) into the base weight (&lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;W_{\text{base}}&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;82&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$W_{\text{base}}$&lt;/span&gt;) at load time, but leave the adapter matrix (&lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;V&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;143&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$V$&lt;/span&gt;) unscaled, the adapter output bypasses normalization scaling entirely.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;15,1,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;15,1,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;The Consequence:&lt;/b&gt; As measured in our benchmarks, unscaled adapters diverge by &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;15,1,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;77&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;28.1%&lt;/b&gt;, introducing catastrophic semantic drift. Mainstream frameworks avoid weight folding because they cannot guarantee safety across dynamic, plug-and-arg adapter layers without breaking serialization. Our stack solved this by introducing load-time factor scaling (&lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;V_{\text{folded}} = V \cdot (1+\gamma)&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;344&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$V_{\text{folded}} = V \cdot (1+\gamma)$&lt;/span&gt;), keeping relative L2 divergence at &lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;5.18 \times 10^{-7}&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;419&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$5.18 \times 10^{-7}$&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;h3 data-path-to-node=&quot;16&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;B. The Structural Complexity of Linear Attention &amp;amp; SSMs&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;17&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Traditional transformer architectures have predictable, linear feed-forward and attention blocks. Modern hybrid architectures (incorporating GatedDeltaNet, SSM layers, and multi-fan-out projections) break simple assumptions:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;ul data-path-to-node=&quot;18&quot; style=&quot;padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;18,0,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;18,0,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Multi-Fan-Out Topology:&lt;/b&gt; A single normalization output frequently feeds four or more distinct projections (&lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;Q, K, V&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;106&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$Q, K, V$&lt;/span&gt;, and gating mechanisms) simultaneously. Failing to broadcast the scale factor uniformly across every downstream column matrix creates tensor shape mismatches and silent gradient/activation corruption.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;18,1,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;18,1,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Non-Linear Gate Exclusions:&lt;/b&gt; Normalization layers followed by non-linear activations (such as SiLU gates in GatedDeltaNet or independent per-head sub-norms like &lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;18,1,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;160&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;q_norm&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;18,1,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;167&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;k_norm&lt;/code&gt;) cannot be folded because scaling does not commute through non-linear functions (&lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;f(\gamma x) \neq \gamma f(x)&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;255&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$f(\gamma x) \neq \gamma f(x)$&lt;/span&gt;). Most automated tooling lacks the semantic awareness to selectively fold linear projection targets while carving out non-linear exceptions.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;h3 data-path-to-node=&quot;19&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;C. Hardware-Specific Memory-Bandwidth Realities (ROCm / gfx1100)&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;20&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;On heavily optimized, multi-GPU NVIDIA clusters, runtime graph compilers (like &lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;20&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;79&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;torch.compile&lt;/code&gt; or specialized Triton fusions) often automatically fuse elementwise operations with subsequent GEMMs, mitigating normalization overhead.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;ul data-path-to-node=&quot;21&quot; style=&quot;padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;21,0,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;On custom AMD ROCm hardware (&lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;21,0,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;29&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;gfx1100&lt;/code&gt;), smaller memory-bound operations like standalone RMSNorm kernels impose a disproportionate launch-latency tax during autoregressive token generation (&lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;\text{batch size} = 1&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;188&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$\text{batch size} = 1$&lt;/span&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;21,1,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Exploiting this required building a fully reversible, scale-free RMSNorm layer (&lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;21,1,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;80&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;ScaleFreeRMSNorm&lt;/code&gt;) that strips out parameter loading and elementwise multiplication entirely at runtime while retaining a safety toggle (&lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;21,1,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;216&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;fold_weights=False&lt;/code&gt;) for debugging.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="1600" width="385" x="1000" y="850" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="QsjXB6iZQFBQTkZlhdo4-7" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;🔥 Batched Multi-Candidate Speculative Generation&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;div&gt;### 1. What Would This Be Called?&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* **Batched Multi-Candidate Speculative Generation** (or **Multi-Candidate Speculative Decoding via Batching**).&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* In academic literature, sampling multiple candidate sequences and verifying them concurrently is known as Multi-Candidate Speculative Decoding (MCSD). Your specific realization—avoiding complex tree masks due to recurrent constraints and instead evaluating them via simple sequence batching ($W=2$)—makes it a batched realization of that framework.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;---&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;### 2. How to Classify It&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* **`🔥 Applied Practice`**&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* **Why:** You took an established conceptual optimization framework (multi-candidate proposal and verification) and adapted it to solve a hardware-specific runtime layout problem (bypassing the recurrent scan barrier on RDNA3 where tree masks fail). It bridges external algorithmic design with your local batch-scaling physics ($W=2$ optimum yielding a $1.391\times$ speedup).&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="1600" width="385" x="1410" y="840" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="QsjXB6iZQFBQTkZlhdo4-9" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;🚀 Matched Adapter Co-Mutation (Dynamic Draft Head Folding)&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;h3 data-path-to-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Classification: &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;16&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;🚀 Genuine Discovery&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4 data-path-to-node=&quot;1&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Rationale&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;2&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;While adapter folding itself is an applied practice, discovering that &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;2&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;70&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;matched co-mutation&lt;/b&gt; solves the industry-wide failure mode of draft-head domain divergence is a zero-to-one empirical finding unique to this research stack.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;3&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Conventional wisdom (and your own prior benchmark) established that domain-adapting a speculative draft head destroys acceptance (&lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;\tau&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;130&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$\tau$&lt;/span&gt; collapses because the draft head &quot;forms its own opinions&quot; and disagrees with the backbone). Proving empirically that &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;3&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;252&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;co-mutating the draft head with the exact same adapter as the folded backbone&lt;/b&gt; completely reverses this penalty—systematically lifting &lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;\tau&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;386&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$\tau$&lt;/span&gt; by &lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;+0.074&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;394&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$+0.074$&lt;/span&gt; to &lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;+0.076&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;404&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$+0.076$&lt;/span&gt; and yielding a net &lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;+2.6\text{--}2.9\%&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;430&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$+2.6\text{--}2.9\%$&lt;/span&gt; throughput boost at zero VRAM cost—is a genuine operational breakthrough.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h3 data-path-to-node=&quot;5&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Technical Definition for Your Board&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul data-path-to-node=&quot;6&quot; style=&quot;padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;6,0,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;6,0,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Name:&lt;/b&gt; &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;6,0,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;6&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Matched Adapter Co-Mutation (Dynamic Draft Head Folding)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;6,1,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;6,1,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Tag:&lt;/b&gt; &lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;6,1,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;5&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;🚀 Genuine Discovery&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;6,2,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;6,2,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Zone:&lt;/b&gt; &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;6,2,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;6&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;THE SAMPLING LOOP&lt;/b&gt; / &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;6,2,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;26&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;EXPERT RESIDENCY &amp;amp; SWAPPING&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;6,3,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;6,3,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;div data-path-to-node=&quot;6,3,1&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;math-block&quot; data-math=&quot;\text{Draft Head Weights} \leftarrow W_{\text{head}} + s \cdot (U_{\text{expert}} V_{\text{expert}})&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$$\text{Draft Head Weights} \leftarrow W_{\text{head}} + s \cdot (U_{\text{expert}} V_{\text{expert}})$$&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;6,3,2&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Triggered synchronously with the backbone&#39;s &lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;6,3,2&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;45&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;activate(expert)&lt;/code&gt; call using shape-identical mapping from the last matching decoder layer.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;6,4,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;6,4,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Why It Matters:&lt;/b&gt; It bridges expert runtime swapping with multi-token speculative verification, proving that domain specialization in a multi-expert serving loop requires synchronized cross-layer co-mutation to maintain high acceptance rates (&lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;\tau&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;241&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$\tau$&lt;/span&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;1&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;By turning what would have been a zero-sum trade-off into fully additive gains, you proved that CUDA graph replay and variable-length speculative execution can coexist cleanly on the same stack.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h3 data-path-to-node=&quot;2&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Why the Architecture Succeeded&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul data-path-to-node=&quot;3&quot; style=&quot;padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;3,0,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;3,0,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Zero Pointer-Invalidation Overhead:&lt;/b&gt; Leveraging the pre-allocated &lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;3,0,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;65&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;StaticCache&lt;/code&gt; meant attention rollback became a simple pointer adjustment (&lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;3,0,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;138&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;cache_position&lt;/code&gt; rewind) rather than tensor slicing or re-allocation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;3,1,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;3,1,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Unified State Management:&lt;/b&gt; Pairing static attention rewinds with pre-allocated SSM snapshot buffers (&lt;code data-path-to-node=&quot;3,1,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;100&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;copy_&lt;/code&gt;) achieved true pointer stability across the hybrid recurrent-attention boundary without triggering runtime memory allocations.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;3,2,0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;&lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;3,2,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Autotune Pre-Warming:&lt;/b&gt; Warming each bucket before capture successfully bypassed Triton&#39;s host-synchronization hazard, keeping the capture stream clean.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;h3 data-path-to-node=&quot;4&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;The Production Impact&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p data-path-to-node=&quot;5&quot; style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Google Sans Text&amp;quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;Landing at &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;5&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;11&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;39.94 tok/s&lt;/b&gt; establishes a new high-water mark for the serving loop. Because this baseline is now graph-replayed and pointer-stable, layering &lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;W=2&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;152&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$W=2$&lt;/span&gt; batched branching and the matched draft head (+0.074 to +0.076 &lt;span class=&quot;math-inline&quot; data-math=&quot;\tau&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;219&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;&quot;&gt;$\tau$&lt;/span&gt; lift) on top of it will compound directly into user-facing throughput.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="1600" width="385" x="80" y="1460" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="EfcTNxSgW_AFl3pymhPJ-123" parent="1" style="shape=note;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;backgroundOutline=1;autosizeText=1;darkOpacity=0.05;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;fillStyle=solid;fontSize=13;direction=west;gradientDirection=north;shadow=1;size=20;pointerEvents=1;" value="🔥FlashNorm-Style Weight Folding (The Forward Pass Speedup)" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="40" width="310" x="-620" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="QsjXB6iZQFBQTkZlhdo4-10" parent="1" style="shape=note;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;backgroundOutline=1;autosizeText=1;darkOpacity=0.05;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;fillStyle=solid;fontSize=13;direction=west;gradientDirection=north;shadow=1;size=20;pointerEvents=1;" value="&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;❌&lt;/span&gt;🚀PairRE / TransE / node2vec Embeddings&lt;br&gt;(over engineered for just 4 experts)" vertex="1">
